WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.641 --> 00:00:08.063
When St.

2
00:00:16.508 --> 00:00:19.110
Rivers left my cottage, it was beginning to snow.

3
00:00:20.311 --> 00:00:23.633
The whirling storm continued all night and the next day.

4
00:00:24.794 --> 00:00:28.516
By twilight the valley was full of drifts and nearly impassable.

5
00:00:29.657 --> 00:00:33.760
For almost an hour, I sat by my hearth listening to the fury of the storm.

6
00:00:35.016 --> 00:00:38.739
At last I lit a candle and began to read my new book of poetry.

7
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.463
I heard a noise and supposed it must be the wind shaking the door.

8
00:00:44.704 --> 00:00:45.585
But it was St.

9
00:00:45.625 --> 00:00:50.809
John, who came in from the howling darkness and stood before me, his cloak as white as a

10
00:00:50.849 --> 00:00:53.331
glacier. "What has happened?"

11
00:00:53.711 --> 00:00:54.332
I demanded.

12
00:00:54.352 --> 00:00:56.373
"Is there bad news?"

13
00:00:56.393 --> 00:01:00.156
"No. How easily alarmed you are."

14
00:01:01.357 --> 00:01:04.140
He hung up his cloak and stamped the snow from his boots.

15
00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:09.483
Warming his hands at the fire, he said, "I have worked hard to get here.

16
00:01:10.143 --> 00:01:11.865
One snowdrift came up to my waist."

17
00:01:11.885 --> 00:01:14.967
"But why are you here?"

18
00:01:14.987 --> 00:01:21.271
"That's a rather inhospitable question to ask a visitor, but since you ask, I'll say simply to have

19
00:01:21.291 --> 00:01:22.272
a little talk with you."

20
00:01:23.532 --> 00:01:24.193
He sat down.

21
00:01:24.213 --> 00:01:27.915
"I grew tired of my silent books and empty rooms.

22
00:01:29.156 --> 00:01:34.280
Besides, since yesterday I've heard half a story, and I'm impatient to hear the rest of it."

23
00:01:35.548 --> 00:01:40.710
I recalled his strange conduct yesterday and began to wonder if he were losing his mind.

24
00:01:41.951 --> 00:01:46.333
But as he began to tell his story, he seemed too calm to be insane.

25
00:01:46.353 --> 00:01:54.576
"Twenty years ago," he said, "a poor clergyman—never mind his name right now—fell in love with a rich

26
00:01:54.616 --> 00:01:55.317
man's daughter.

27
00:01:56.537 --> 00:02:02.460
She married him against the advice of all her friends and family, who consequently disowned her right after

28
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:02.900
the wedding.

29
00:02:04.168 --> 00:02:08.592
Within two years they were dead, and their infant daughter was sent to live with an aunt and

30
00:02:08.732 --> 00:02:10.594
uncle named Reed of Gateshead."

31
00:02:12.676 --> 00:02:13.917
I started in surprise.

32
00:02:13.937 --> 00:02:15.698
"What's wrong?

33
00:02:16.859 --> 00:02:17.680
Did you hear something?"

34
00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:18.541
he asked me.

35
00:02:18.561 --> 00:02:21.543
"It was probably just a mouse in the walls.

36
00:02:22.805 --> 00:02:24.746
To go on after Mr.

37
00:02:24.786 --> 00:02:29.791
Reed died, his wife kept the orphan for ten years and then sent her to Lowood, where you

38
00:02:29.831 --> 00:02:30.952
resided for so long.

39
00:02:32.308 --> 00:02:37.411
What strikes me is how many similarities there are between this orphan's story and yours.

40
00:02:38.872 --> 00:02:42.014
She left Lowood to become a governess at the home of a Mr.

41
00:02:42.094 --> 00:02:44.815
Rochester." "Please tell me, Mr.

42
00:02:44.855 --> 00:02:48.778
Rivers—" "I'm almost finished," he interrupted.

43
00:02:48.798 --> 00:02:50.959
"I know nothing of Mr.

44
00:02:50.999 --> 00:02:56.102
Rochester's character except that he proposed to this girl, and at the altar, she discovered that he was

45
00:02:56.162 --> 00:02:59.064
already married to another woman, who was insane.

46
00:03:00.412 --> 00:03:01.532
Sometime later Mr.

47
00:03:01.612 --> 00:03:07.994
Briggs, a London solicitor, needed to contact this governess, but she had fled Thornfield, and no one knew

48
00:03:08.034 --> 00:03:08.874
where to find her.

49
00:03:10.955 --> 00:03:12.955
Isn't that the strangest tale?"

50
00:03:12.975 --> 00:03:17.577
"Since you know so much, just tell me this," I said firmly.

51
00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:19.457
"Where is Mr.

52
00:03:19.497 --> 00:03:21.878
Rochester? What is he doing?

53
00:03:22.958 --> 00:03:23.498
Is he well?"

54
00:03:23.518 --> 00:03:25.619
"I know nothing of Mr.

55
00:03:25.659 --> 00:03:28.320
Rochester. I only know what Mr.

56
00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:29.660
Briggs wrote to me in his letter.

57
00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:33.521
You should ask me the name of the governess and why Mr.

58
00:03:33.561 --> 00:03:34.702
Briggs was looking for her."

59
00:03:35.862 --> 00:03:38.343
But I only wanted to hear about Edward Rochester.

60
00:03:38.363 --> 00:03:41.264
"Didn't anyone go to Thornfield?

61
00:03:41.544 --> 00:03:43.105
Didn't anyone write to Mr.

62
00:03:43.145 --> 00:03:45.666
Rochester?" "Of course they wrote."

63
00:03:45.686 --> 00:03:47.827
"And what did he reply?"

64
00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:49.267
I asked impatiently.

65
00:03:49.287 --> 00:03:53.189
"Mr. Briggs said that his letters were answered not by Mr.

66
00:03:53.249 --> 00:03:54.770
Rochester but by a Mrs.

67
00:03:54.830 --> 00:04:00.532
Fairfax." I felt cold and dismayed because my worst fears had probably come true.

68
00:04:02.548 --> 00:04:06.610
Mr. Rochester had left England and rushed abroad in reckless desperation.

69
00:04:07.911 --> 00:04:09.131
Oh, my poor master!

70
00:04:10.332 --> 00:04:11.372
Oh, my poor Edward!

71
00:04:11.392 --> 00:04:14.674
"He must have been a bad man," said St.

72
00:04:14.714 --> 00:04:18.776
John. "You don't know him, so don't judge him," I snapped.

73
00:04:18.796 --> 00:04:21.157
"Very well," St.

74
00:04:21.177 --> 00:04:22.078
John said quietly.

75
00:04:22.098 --> 00:04:26.780
"Since you won't ask the name of the governess, I'll show it to you in black and white."

76
00:04:28.072 --> 00:04:32.914
From his pocket he produced a shabby slip of paper that I recognized as the one he'd torn

77
00:04:32.974 --> 00:04:34.475
from my cover sheet yesterday.

78
00:04:35.635 --> 00:04:38.097
He got up and held the paper close to my eyes.

79
00:04:39.317 --> 00:04:46.220
There I saw in my own handwriting the words "Jane Eyre," doubtless the result of some daydreaming.

80
00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:49.422
"Briggs wrote to me of a Jane Eyre.

81
00:04:50.662 --> 00:04:54.624
I know a Jane Elliott, though I confess I have my suspicions about you.

82
00:04:56.536 --> 00:04:58.377
Do you admit that Jane Eyre is your name?"

83
00:04:58.397 --> 00:05:02.559
"Yes, yes," I said quickly, "but where is Mr.

84
00:05:02.579 --> 00:05:05.441
Briggs? Perhaps he knows more of Mr.

85
00:05:05.481 --> 00:05:06.481
Rochester than you do."

86
00:05:06.501 --> 00:05:10.504
"Briggs is in London, but you haven't asked what he wanted with you."

87
00:05:10.524 --> 00:05:12.685
"Well, what did he want?"

88
00:05:12.705 --> 00:05:16.367
"Merely to tell you that your uncle, Mr.

89
00:05:16.447 --> 00:05:21.229
John Eyre of Madeira, is dead and that he left you all his property and that you are

90
00:05:21.249 --> 00:05:21.850
now rich."

91
00:05:23.291 --> 00:05:24.091
I was stunned.

92
00:05:24.111 --> 00:05:26.720
"Me! Rich?"

93
00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:29.361
"Yes, you're an heiress."

94
00:05:30.661 --> 00:05:33.342
I was so surprised that for a few minutes I was silent.

95
00:05:34.542 --> 00:05:37.803
Finally I said, "How much am I worth?"

96
00:05:39.064 --> 00:05:40.904
"Twenty thousand pounds."

97
00:05:42.144 --> 00:05:44.165
"Twenty thousand pounds?"

98
00:05:45.425 --> 00:05:50.427
This was a new stunner because I'd been thinking I'd inherited perhaps four or five thousand.

99
00:05:52.367 --> 00:05:55.168
The true amount actually took my breath away for a few minutes.

100
00:05:56.508 --> 00:05:57.628
I had never seen St.

101
00:05:57.648 --> 00:05:59.789
John laugh, but he was laughing now.

102
00:05:59.809 --> 00:06:05.091
"Well, if you'd committed a murder and been caught, you couldn't look more shocked than you do at

103
00:06:05.111 --> 00:06:05.711
this moment."

104
00:06:06.911 --> 00:06:08.712
He put on his cloak and began to leave.

105
00:06:08.732 --> 00:06:11.492
"Wait! Why did Mr.

106
00:06:11.532 --> 00:06:12.533
Briggs contact you?

107
00:06:13.233 --> 00:06:16.774
Why did he think that you, living in this remote place, could help find me?"

108
00:06:16.794 --> 00:06:21.215
"Oh, the clergy are often appealed to about odd matters."

109
00:06:22.556 --> 00:06:23.416
He lifted the latch.

110
00:06:24.652 --> 00:06:27.934
But his answer did not satisfy me, and I demanded to know more.

111
00:06:29.115 --> 00:06:32.677
With some reluctance he told me that his name was St.

112
00:06:32.697 --> 00:06:40.021
John Eyre Rivers and that his mother had two brothers, my father and John Eyre of Madeira.

113
00:06:40.041 --> 00:06:43.743
"So you and Diana and Mary are my cousins!"

114
00:06:44.184 --> 00:06:44.924
I exclaimed.

115
00:06:44.944 --> 00:06:47.465
"Oh, I'm so glad!"

116
00:06:47.485 --> 00:06:51.488
St. John smiled at my excitement.

117
00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:58.144
"You looked serious when I told you about your fortune, but now you're excited about something insignificant."

118
00:06:58.164 --> 00:07:04.688
"It may mean nothing to you, who have two sisters, but I had nobody, and now I have

119
00:07:04.768 --> 00:07:05.769
three relatives.

120
00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:11.093
I will regard you as my brother, and Diana and Mary as my sisters."

121
00:07:12.433 --> 00:07:17.917
I paced the room because suddenly I had an idea: I would divide my fortune among the four

122
00:07:17.957 --> 00:07:19.978
of us, and I would live at Moor House.

123
00:07:21.379 --> 00:07:21.820
"Oh, no!"

124
00:07:22.448 --> 00:07:24.570
St. John protested when he heard my plan.

125
00:07:24.591 --> 00:07:26.312
"You are acting on impulse."

126
00:07:27.574 --> 00:07:29.255
But I was firm in my resolve.

127
00:07:30.417 --> 00:07:35.102
I would repay my rescuers, and we would each have five thousand pounds.
